    {
      "category": "Cat 2",
      "claim": "Section templates + length bounds control output cost",
      "measured": "same model (Sonnet): \u221254%; + Haiku: \u221298%; 1-section regen vs full re-draft: \u221249%",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "note": "The cost claim holds, but the saving is not free: graded blind, the templated drafts score lower on accuracy (3.92 \u2192 3.08 / 2.58) and have more serious errors (1 \u2192 6 / 10); see the quality table and the implicit-claim row."
    },
    {
      "category": "Cat 2",
      "claim": "\u2026without losing draft quality (implicit)",
      "measured": "head-to-head: templated drafts preferred over the unbounded draft in 0/16 comparisons (0 ties; 95% Wilson 0\u201319%); accuracy 3.92 unbounded vs 3.08 / 2.58 (Sonnet / Haiku template), coverage 4.92 vs 4.00 / 3.50; serious errors 1 vs 6 / 10. Not worse: core facts 32/32 vs 32/32 / 32/32. Better: clarity 4.00 vs 4.92 / 4.42, concision 2.83 vs 4.17 / 4.08",
      "verdict": "NOT SUPPORTED",
      "note": "Blind LLM judge (`claude-opus-5-5`, not one of the models under test; 3 rubric passes per draft; every pair shown in both orders, position-consistent in 11/12 pairs), one draft per rung \u00d7 4 documents; the lead verified the serious errors against the source payloads. Shorter drafts keep every core fact and read better, but they are less accurate and less complete and lose every head-to-head comparison. The template requires an owner and a time window for every action item; the grader flagged an owner or deadline the source does not support in 16 of 24 templated action items (12 with a serious error). A single judge may favour longer drafts (its prompt tells it to ignore length and layout), but the serious errors were verified against the sources, so they do not rest on the judge's taste."
    },
    {
      "category": "Tiers 1\u20132 rule",
      "claim": "60\u201380% lower unit inference cost (Cat 1 & 2)",
      "measured": "Cat 1 \u221299%, Cat 2 \u221254% (template only) to \u221298% (template + Haiku)",
      "verdict": "PARTLY SUPPORTED",
      "note": "Outside the range on both sides: changing the model as well gives ~98\u201399% (more than claimed); templating alone on the same model gives 54% (less)."
    },
    {
